Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["6 Cantor\[CloseCurlyQuote]s Enumeration & the Failure of Set-Builder \
Notation", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.845314312800625*^9, 3.8453143269983635`*^9}, {
   3.851975433052054*^9, 3.851975443510944*^9}, {3.852757434293027*^9, 
   3.852757443292274*^9}, {3.852839292986291*^9, 3.852839312457459*^9}, {
   3.852839745759389*^9, 3.852839760112888*^9}, {3.92340720757247*^9, 
   3.923407210687661*^9}, {3.923407636918423*^9, 3.923407694038988*^9}, {
   3.931274124428792*^9, 3.931274130417455*^9}, {3.931928969164657*^9, 
   3.931928971262286*^9}, {3.935676344174761*^9, 3.935676358124407*^9}, {
   3.936039196385202*^9, 3.9360392223922825`*^9}, {3.936127630055807*^9, 
   3.936127669735153*^9}, {3.936130940340014*^9, 3.93613094168902*^9}, {
   3.936131043765196*^9, 3.936131058245112*^9}, 3.9361367136709957`*^9, {
   3.9419884977457924`*^9, 3.94198854698242*^9}, {3.941989035450865*^9, 
   3.941989036207339*^9}, {3.941989072772583*^9, 3.941989083999554*^9}, {
   3.9442488054873238`*^9, 3.944248806139517*^9}, {3.946556353384184*^9, 
   3.946556353659445*^9}, 
   3.967652180952776*^9},ExpressionUUID->"9d1dca92-3287-3d46-beb3-\
888080e46a1f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Finally, let\[CloseCurlyQuote]s consider an example that will resonate with \
mathematicians: the IC notation construction of the first enumeration of the \
rationals, published in (Cantor, 1874). ",
 "Georg",
 StyleBox[" ",
  FontWeight->"Bold"],
 "Cantor, the father of transfinite mathematics, published his proof of the \
countability of the rationals in 1874, over 150 years ago. One might presume \
that in the intervening time someone would have come up with a concise, \
mathematically precise way to describe the process. Sadly, that expectation \
would be incorrect. Words to describe Cantor\[CloseCurlyQuote]s method are \
easy:"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.942069697461191*^9, 3.942069707057463*^9}, {
   3.9422557441813755`*^9, 3.94225589334108*^9}, {3.9422577924295864`*^9, 
   3.942257796200889*^9}, {3.942259423842796*^9, 3.9422594555834103`*^9}, {
   3.942259958033214*^9, 3.942260088728725*^9}, {3.942262545621668*^9, 
   3.942262586621893*^9}, {3.942437604754925*^9, 3.942437607483881*^9}, 
   3.94276915907843*^9, {3.943271452775858*^9, 3.943271494644155*^9}, {
   3.9454610469401016`*^9, 3.945461130248823*^9}, {3.94655642173455*^9, 
   3.946556452160937*^9}, {3.947681805037541*^9, 
   3.947681811676281*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"d58595a0-4ef1-7843-b021-\
b3c56606f3da"],

Cell["\<\
Imagine an infinite table in which the first element of the first row is 1/1, \
and then moving one column to the right always increases the numerator by 1, \
while moving one row down increases the denominator by 1.\
\>", "ItemNumbered",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.942069697461191*^9, 3.942069707057463*^9}, {
  3.9422557441813755`*^9, 3.9422558933410816`*^9}, {3.9422577924295864`*^9, 
  3.942257796200889*^9}, {3.942259423842796*^9, 3.9422594555834103`*^9}, {
  3.942259958033214*^9, 3.942260087504711*^9}, {3.942260157597328*^9, 
  3.942260400395607*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"e61c67dc-a3c8-c94a-b609-\
8993f103e832"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Following any (infinite) row or column would mean never getting to the next \
one; instead, we traverse the array one (finite) diagonal at a time, starting \
from the upper left corner. Note that for all elements ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"n", "/", "d"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "62cd5cf1-a865-5245-89cd-c0a0e001cf67"],
 " on a given diagonal, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"n", "+", "d"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "e564d1ab-d111-8f45-aedb-2d136137f99e"],
 " is a constant, and in fact, on diagonal number ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   StyleBox["diag",
    FontSlant->"Italic"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "97824abf-1867-814e-be94-54ea5b2347b5"],
 ", ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{
    RowBox[{"n", "+", "d"}], "=", 
    RowBox[{
     StyleBox["diag",
      FontSlant->"Italic"], "+", "1"}]}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "db458465-869c-1546-9669-85cc80d067da"],
 "."
}], "ItemNumbered",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.942069697461191*^9, 3.942069707057463*^9}, {
   3.9422557441813755`*^9, 3.94225589334108*^9}, {3.9422577924295864`*^9, 
   3.942257796200889*^9}, {3.942259423842796*^9, 3.9422594555834103`*^9}, {
   3.942259958033214*^9, 3.942260087504711*^9}, {3.942260157597328*^9, 
   3.9422605006622047`*^9}, {3.942260534538999*^9, 3.942260629223385*^9}, 
   3.9465566133113594`*^9, {3.9478598527540836`*^9, 
   3.947859854031271*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"eca28a67-7d9e-5948-9f57-\
3de683502d5e"],

Cell["\<\
Cantor actually used a \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]snake-like\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] traversal, winding back-and-forth on alternate \
diagonals, but for our purposes a \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]trace and retrace\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] pattern is preferable.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.947859862094839*^9},ExpressionUUID->"f48febe2-7f5a-544a-b3a9-\
130f713e7adf"],

Cell["\<\
Figure 2: A traversal producing Cantor\[CloseCurlyQuote]s enumeration of \
fractions.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.947682786533825*^9, 
  3.947682788159037*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"6c27dacc-8a64-3848-9702-\
094783df546f"],

