Cell[TextData[{
 "Expanding the outer IC levels shows a fascinating feature of the IC \
notation. Although by construction all graph edges connect lower numbered \
vertices to higher numbered ones, so the EDSL contains only positive numbers, \
a 0 appeared in the final IC for ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"n", "=", "1"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "6bc72bf4-8bbe-014c-9d35-436099d5312d"],
 ", but only as the ending value for the index: ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  RowBox[{
   UnderoverscriptBox["\[Euro]", 
    RowBox[{"j", "\[DoubleRightTee]", "1"}], "0"], "[", 
   RowBox[{"{", "1", "}"}], "]"}]],
  CellGroupingRules->{"GroupTogetherGrouping", 10000.},
  CellChangeTimes->{{3.941984219869797*^9, 3.941984239405833*^9}, 
    3.9419843192935715`*^9},ExpressionUUID->
  "eb39a857-441c-b441-9bfa-4b2113996790"],
 ". Further examples showed that a 0 or even a negative ending value is \
perfectly legal for an indexed concatenation: That subsequence is simply \
omitted from the fully expanded list. Similarly, ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  TagBox[
   RowBox[{
    UnderoverscriptBox["\[Euro]", 
     RowBox[{"i", "\[DoubleRightTee]", "1"}], "1"], "[", 
    RowBox[{"{", "1", "}"}], "]"}],
   DisplayForm]],
  CellGroupingRules->{"GroupTogetherGrouping", 10000.},
  CellChangeTimes->{{3.941984219869797*^9, 3.941984239405833*^9}, 
    3.9419843192935715`*^9},ExpressionUUID->
  "0d4ddca8-c69a-7b4c-8f69-131ba7624e5d"],
 " only expands out to a single copy of ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   RowBox[{"{", "1", "}"}], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "5b91c3f7-9a22-8e49-8738-a441c8780cd8"],
 "."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.941984525202671*^9, 3.941984874318613*^9}, {
   3.9419851482002087`*^9, 3.941985149735763*^9}, {3.942069572388273*^9, 
   3.9420695955562363`*^9}, {3.942076638788793*^9, 3.94207668735424*^9}, 
   3.942229415942798*^9, 3.942241238700337*^9, {3.9477685740477943`*^9, 
   3.9477685799097023`*^9}, 
   3.947862158414368*^9},ExpressionUUID->"b4869f93-523d-8943-8ef7-\
78dffc0ccd45"],

